# Two wrappers for the schwa, which is a phoneme of Catalan's own and so has
# none of IBM's. Both are the shape of the 1,037 the module already has: bare,
# so there is no landing place and no ventproc, because a wrapper is entered
# and answers and the choice points around the call belong to whoever planted
# them.
#
# The two they stand beside are ZZtest_string_s_4_1_ZZstring115 and
# ZZlprp_load__insert_2pt_s_4_1_ZZstring115, which carry the phone a; the 4
# is the field the string belongs to and the 1 is how many.

# Whether the phone the scan is on is the schwa.
rule cat_test_schwa takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_schwa
end

# Lay a schwa down between the two ends a letter rule was handed.
rule cat_insert_schwa takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_schwa 0
end


# ---- the two open vowels -------------------------------------------------
#
# Getting a grave accent to a rule of its own takes three wrappers, because
# the module has nowhere for one to go. normalize_span_letters flattens every
# character Castilian does not use, and the letter chain has no arm for one
# that survives, so a grave either loses its accent or goes silent. Both are
# reached through wrappers used in exactly one place each, which is what makes
# this cheap: a wrapper is a rule, and a rule of ours in its place is an arm
# taken out of one chain and put into another.

# The two arms of the normaliser that flatten e and o with a grave. Answering
# 1 is what test_string_s answers when the character is not the one asked
# about, so the arm is never taken and the grave comes through as itself.
rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring164 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end

rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring9 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end

# And the arm of the letter chain that Castilian keeps for n with a tilde,
# which Catalan never writes: it spells that sound ny, and apply_span_n_rules
# is where that is done. So the arm is free, and this is what it now asks --
# is the letter either grave? -- with apply_span_enye_rules, which it calls,
# rewritten to say which.
rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring58 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_egrave
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_ograve
end

# The open vowels as phones, for the value chain to test and a letter rule to
# lay down. The same shape as the schwa's pair.
rule cat_test_eopen takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_eopen
end

rule cat_test_oopen takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_oopen
end

rule cat_insert_eopen takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_eopen 0
end

rule cat_insert_oopen takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_oopen 0
end

# Whether the letter the scan is on is one Catalan says without voice. A
# stop before one of these devoices exactly as it does at the end of a word --
# dissabte is [d i s a p t @], dubte [d u p t @], capser [k a p s e] -- and
# the three stop rules ask this once rather than carrying five tests each.
#
# The caller sets the scan; a test that fails leaves it where it was, so the
# five can be asked one after another. Answering 0 is a match, which is what
# test_string_s answers and what the rules that call this read.
rule cat_voiceless_follows takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring104                 # t
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring96                  # s
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring20                  # c
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring90                  # p
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring48                  # f
end

# Nothing at all, laid down where Castilian lays down a joiner.
#
# Castilian writes a two-digit number as three words -- cuarenta y cinco --
# and convert_span_double puts the space and the y in between with two calls.
# Catalan writes the same number as one: quarantacinc, vinticinc. So the two
# call sites in that rule are pointed at these, which take what they are given
# and lay down nothing, and the tens word and the unit meet with no boundary
# between them.
#
# The first lays down the mark alone, which joins the two halves without a
# space and keeps the rules that ask about neighbouring letters from reading
# across it. The second lays down nothing, which is where the y went.
rule cat_insert_join takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call insert_l 1 1 sym cat_num_join 0
end

rule cat_insert_nothing_given takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 0
end


# ---- the arm that made an ll into a y ------------------------------------

# Castilian is yeista: its ll is /j/ and between two vowels it is the
# approximant, so span_phrase_level_rules has an arm that rewrites the phone L
# into y when the word before ends in a sonorant. Catalan's ll is a lateral
# wherever it stands -- la llum is [l @ L u m] and not [l @ y u m], el llop
# [@ l L O p] -- and that arm was making it a y at every word boundary after a
# vowel, which is most of the times the language writes one.
#
# The arm is reached through this wrapper and through no other call in the
# module, so a wrapper that never matches is that arm taken out of the chain,
# the same way the two graves were freed above. Answering 1 is what test_synch
# answers when the mark it is asked about is not there.
rule ZZtest_synch20_ZZstring77 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end


# ---- a word-final r, which Central Catalan does not say -------------------

# passar is [p @ s a], carrer [k @ R e], amor [@ m u]; clar, cor and mar keep
# theirs. What tells the two apart is not the ending but the word: an r at the
# end goes when there is a second vowel behind it, which is why every one of
# the three that keep it is a syllable long.
#
# The letters are where this can be asked, because a letter rule is handed the
# run it is reading and the scan will walk either way from it. What cannot be
# done there is drop the r outright: assign_span_stress decides between the
# last syllable and the one before it by looking at the last phone, and a word
# that ended in a vowel would take the stress a syllable back -- passar came
# out [p a s @], which is a different word. So the r becomes the phone
# nothing here can reach instead, and ss_val.up makes that silent: the word
# still ends in a consonant when the stress is placed, and says nothing when
# it is spoken.

# Whether the r the letter rule is reading is the last sound of its word.
# Three things count as the end: nothing to the right at all, something that
# is not a letter, or an s that is itself the last -- carrers is [k @ R e s]
# and dinars [d i n a s], because the plural is added to a word that had
# already lost its r.
rule cat_word_end takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 1
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # a letter after it?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring96                  # an s?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # and nothing after that?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# One step of the walk leftwards over the letters: nought where the step
# found a vowel and one where it did not. A test that matches leaves the scan
# where it was, so the letter has to be stepped over by hand; advance_tok is
# what does that, and it answers the other way round from the tests, nought
# for a step taken.
#
# A step that has run out of word does not move, so asking again asks about
# the same place and answers the same. That is what lets the caller below
# repeat this a fixed number of times without having to be told to stop.
rule cat_vowel_step takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # still inside the word?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_4_1                                # a vowel?
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call advance_tok
  answer 1
end

# Whether there is another vowel behind the one the caller has stopped on.
# Written out rather than looped: the notation's while is a backtracking loop
# and wants a choice point, which a bare rule has no business planting -- the
# ones around the call belong to the letter rule that made it. Eight steps is
# further than any Catalan word puts two vowels apart; constructor has three
# letters between its last two and transgressor three.
rule cat_more_vowels_left takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# The phone a dropped r becomes, laid down between the two ends the letter
# rule was handed. The same shape as the schwa's inserter above.
rule cat_insert_mute takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_mute 0
end
